🎮 Act I: The Guardian’s Call

The Guardian slammed the door behind him and leaned against it, gasping for breath.

“Holy shit. Holy absolute shit.”

His house was a wreck. Maps covered the table. His old sword lay beside a half-empty backpack, and his armor was missing half its straps.

He looked at the sword.

Then at the backpack.

Then back at the sword.

“That’s what I’ve got? That’s my equipment? I’m supposed to fight an entire army with this piece of crap?”

Outside, thunder rolled over the mountains.

Far to the north, the Blood Castle stood against a sky the color of dried blood. Its towers were crowded with lizard warriors, their yellow eyes shining through the darkness.

Inside the throne room, the Dark Lord watched his generals gather.

“Those pathetic heroes think they can stop me,” he growled. “Send the hunting parties. I want every road watched and every village searched.”

His mistress rose from her throne beside him. Her black armor gleamed beneath the red firelight.

“And when we find them,” she said, smiling, “I’ll deal with them personally.”

The Dark Lord turned toward her.

“Last time you said that, you lost an entire battalion.”

She narrowed her eyes.

“Keep talking.”

“Just get the army ready.”

Below the throne room, hundreds of lizard warriors hammered their weapons against their shields. Their war drums shook dust from the ceiling.

The invasion had begun.

Meanwhile, the Guardian shoved supplies into his backpack.

“Right. Food. Water. Bandages. Weapons. Something that doesn’t fall apart when a monster looks at it.”

He stopped.

“All right. Where the hell am I supposed to get any of that?”

A knock came from the back door.

He froze.

Another knock.

Then a voice.

“If you’re finished arguing with your backpack, open the damn door.”

The Guardian grabbed his sword and approached cautiously.

“Who are you?”

“Someone who knows where to find weapons.”

“Are you armed?”

“Obviously.”

“Are you dangerous?”

A pause.

“To the right people.”

The Guardian opened the door.

Standing in the rain was a tall woman wearing battered silver armor. A longbow rested across her back, and a pair of curved blades hung at her waist. Behind her stood a hulking figure carrying a hammer large enough to break a wagon in half.

The woman studied the Guardian’s equipment.

“You’re going to need help.”

“No kidding.”

“You’re going to need better weapons.”

“Tell me something I don’t know.”

“And you’re going to need a team.”

The Guardian looked past her at the enormous figure holding the hammer.

“Does he talk?”

The giant grinned.

“Sometimes.”

“Great. Does he eat people?”

“Only if they deserve it.”

The Guardian sighed and grabbed his backpack.

“Fine. I’m in. But before we go anywhere, we’re stopping for supplies.”

The woman nodded toward the northern road.

“Then we’d better hurry. The Blood Castle has already sent its hunters.”

A distant howl rolled across the valley.

The giant lifted his hammer.

“Too late.”

Something moved between the trees.

Then something else.

The Guardian drew his sword.

“Oh, you’ve got to be fucking kidding me.”

The first monster burst from the darkness.

